# Wavefront OBJ export of the canonical Cornell scene
# (webgpu-previz / oracle-mitsuba / export_cornell_obj.py)
#
# Source mesh : cornell_triangles.build_cornell_triangles(default_scene())
#               — the SAME tessellated mesh consumed by the Mitsuba
#               oracle and the WebGPU candidate (Bug B17 closure).
# Scene hash  : d04cf4f99bcd3e7ab619f58df23d2d8e2c59cc7ace0dad71a7344505ffcba93f (scene_schema.hash_scene, canonical JSON SHA-256)
# Mesh stats  : 1146 vertices, 2060 triangles, 8 materials (mat_0..mat_7)
# Spheres     : UV-tessellated, 16 latitude x 32 longitude segments,
#               smooth per-vertex normals (not flat per-face).
#
# WINDING / FACING CONVENTION — preserved EXACTLY from the canonical mesh:
#   * Face indices are copied 1:1 from the canonical index buffer
#     (quads: p0,p1,p2 then p0,p2,p3; spheres: v0,v1,v2 then v0,v2,v3).
#   * Triangles are CCW front-facing viewed from the box INTERIOR:
#     wall normals point into the box, light-quad normal is (0,-1,0),
#     sphere normals point outward.
#   * The WebGPU rasterizer culls back faces (CCW front, Y-down
#     framebuffer); the path tracer ignores winding. Either way:
#     do NOT flip/reorder indices when re-importing — Blender renders
#     both faces by default, so canonical winding == WebGPU-visible
#     geometry == Mitsuba geometry.
#
# Coordinates  : right-handed, Y up, +Z toward the viewer, meters.
#               Matches the OBJ convention — no axis flip on import.
# Camera       : canonical eye (0, 0.6, 3.2) looks -Z through the open
#               front face (the box has no wall at z = +size).
# Determinism  : %.9g formatting (float32-exact round-trip), canonical
#               vertex/face order, mat-id group order. Byte-stable.
#
# Faces are grouped by material (g mat_<id> + usemtl) so importers can
# split the mesh by material. JSON sidecar (source of truth):
#   cornell.obj.scene.json
mtllib cornell.mtl
